+    /* RADIANCE: `add_bos` HAS TO COME FROM THE INPUTS, OR THE INPUT FIELD IS DEAD.
+     *
+     * `common_chat_templates_inputs` carries add_bos/add_eos and every caller fills them, but
+     * this conversion overwrote both from the TEMPLATES object -- which is only ever set inside
+     * common_chat_templates_init, from a llama_vocab. A host that passes `model = nullptr` and
+     * supplies the vocabulary's facts as overrides (which is what radiance does: the container
+     * carries the template, the BOS/EOS spellings and the add flags, so there is no llama_model
+     * to read them off) therefore had both flags stuck at false no matter what it asked for.
+     *
+     * What the flag does is strip a BOS the TEMPLATE rendered, because the tokeniser is about to
+     * add one -- so with it stuck off, a container whose tokeniser adds BOS and whose template
+     * also writes `bos_token` gets TWO. The Llama-family templates write it; Qwen's do not,
+     * which is why this stayed invisible here.
+     *
+     * The two upstream callers that do set it -- common_chat_format_single and
+     * common_chat_format_example -- assign `inputs.add_bos = tmpls->add_bos` immediately before
+     * calling apply, which is exactly the redundancy you would expect if apply were meant to read
+     * the inputs. Falling back to the templates object keeps them working unchanged. */
+    params.add_bos               = inputs.add_bos || tmpls->add_bos;
+    params.add_eos               = inputs.add_eos || tmpls->add_eos;
 
